<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/distributed-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A distributed system is many machines cooperating as one — and it must work
    <strong>while parts of it are broken, slow, or lying</strong>. Peter Deutsch catalogued the
    traps ("the fallacies of distributed computing"): the network is <em>not</em> reliable, latency
    is <em>not</em> zero, and topology <em>does</em> change. Everything in this subject is a
    disciplined answer to those sentences. The reason the discipline exists is arithmetic: a single
    machine hits hard ceilings — one 8-core server is one 8-core server forever — while 100 modest
    machines give you 100× the capacity, 100× the failure surface, and a new class of problems
    that no single-machine program ever meets: messages lost mid-flight, two servers claiming the
    same booking, a network split leaving both halves alive and both serving users.
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert move is to treat these not as bugs to fix but as physics to design around. You
    cannot have everything: reads that are always right <em>and</em> a system that answers during
    a network cut are mutually exclusive, provably. The craft is choosing, per piece of data, how
    much disagreement you can afford — and then building machinery that keeps the disagreement
    inside the bounds you chose.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five ideas that define the field" widget="equationStep" config={distCfg} />

  <h2>The internet and the web: the original distributed system</h2>
  <p>
    The internet is the field's existence proof: billions of unreliable links cooperating into one
    network since 1983. Its mechanisms repay study because every datacentre reuses them.
    <strong>Packet switching</strong> slices messages into independent packets that route
    separately and reassemble at the destination — no dedicated circuit needed, so the network
    survives losing entire links (that was its Cold War design goal). <strong>DNS</strong> is a
    hierarchical, eventually-consistent database translating names to addresses, cached for hours
    or days: the textbook case of accepting staleness in exchange for availability. <strong>BGP</strong>
    lets each network announce which neighbours can reach which ranges of addresses, with no
    global authority. And the web itself — HTTP's stateless request–response over that network —
    is the design decision that let it scale to a trillion pages: every request stands alone, so
    any server can answer any request.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why it matters: when the mechanisms fail, the world watches. On 4 October 2021, Facebook
    withdrew the BGP announcements that told the internet how to reach its DNS servers; for about
    six hours Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp vanished — and the company's own staff could not
    easily reach the buildings or tools to fix it, because internal routes went down with the
    announcement. One protocol-level mistake, planet-sized outage: the distributed-system lesson
    in one afternoon, and the reason serious teams treat routing and DNS as safety-critical.
  </p>

  <h2>Partitions, replication and the CAP choice</h2>
  <p>
    Now make it concrete with the two moves every distributed database makes. <strong>Sharding</strong>
    splits data across machines (user IDs ending 0–4 to node 1, 5–9 to node 2), turning one
    overloaded disk into four quiet ones. <strong>Replication</strong> copies each shard to two or
    three nodes so a crashed machine loses nothing. The price appears immediately: with three
    copies, a write to one leaves the other two stale — so what does a read see? Brewer's CAP
    theorem forces the choice during any network partition: you can stay <em>consistent</em>
    (refuse reads and writes that cannot reach a quorum) or stay <em>available</em> (answer from
    whatever copy you have), never both. Production systems operationalise it with quorums: with
    N = 3 replicas, if writes need W = 2 acknowledgements and reads wait for R = 2 responses, then
    W + R = 4 exceeds N, so every read must overlap at least one fresh copy — consistency
    survives one crashed node, at the cost of every read contacting two machines.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "CAP means you permanently give up one of the three
    properties." The choice only bites <em>during a partition</em>, and it is per-operation, not
    per-system: a bank runs strongly consistent on balances but eventually consistent on
    recommendations, and a well-built store lets you tune W and R per request. Engineers who
    misread CAP as an excuse for one-mode-for-everything build systems that are either slow
    everywhere or wrong where it counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Digital currency: consensus without a referee</h2>
  <p>
    Money is the hardest distributed-systems problem dressed in economics: everyone must agree on
    one ledger, and nobody may double-spend. Centralised systems solve it with a referee (your
    bank's database is the single source of truth). Bitcoin (2008) solved it without one, and the
    mechanism is the part worth knowing. Transactions broadcast to a peer-to-peer network;
    <strong>miners</strong> bundle them into blocks and compete to hash the block header
    repeatedly until the result falls below a target — on current hardware roughly 10
    <sup>20</sup> hashes per network per second, so finding each block is about 10 minutes of
    global luck. The winner's block becomes the next page of the ledger, chained to the last by
    its hash, and rewriting history means re-mining every block since — economically
    self-defeating against an honest majority. This is the Byzantine Generals problem cracked by
    proof-of-work: agreement among parties who cannot trust each other or any messenger.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade-offs are the distributed-systems trade-offs wearing a currency: the network
    processes on the order of 7 transactions per second (Visa peaks in the tens of thousands) and
    each consumes more energy than many households use in a year, which is why Ethereum switched
    to proof-of-stake in 2022 — validators stake coins instead of burning electricity, cutting its
    energy use by an estimated 99.9%. And "immutable" has an asterisk: a cartel controlling most
    of the hash power can reorganise recent blocks (51% attacks on smaller coins are documented),
    so the guarantees are economic and probabilistic, not mathematical absolutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "blockchain is immutable because of cryptography." Hashes and
    signatures make tampering <em>detectable and expensive</em>; immutability comes from economics
    (re-mining history costs more than it can steal) plus the social layer — the community can and
    does fork away from ledger states it rejects, as Ethereum did after the 2016 DAO hack. A
    system you can fork is a system whose immutability is political, not physical.
  </p>

  <h2>Cyber security in a system with no perimeter</h2>
  <p>
    Distributing machines distributes the attack surface, so security stops being a wall and
    becomes a property of every hop. The mechanisms, in order of what they protect:
    <strong>TLS</strong> encrypts each connection and authenticates servers via certificate
    chains — it protects data on the wire, nothing more; <strong>authentication and
    authorisation</strong> between services (mutual TLS, signed tokens) protect
    service-to-service calls once attackers are inside the network, the assumption behind
    "zero-trust" architectures; <strong>rate limiting and idempotency keys</strong> protect
    against replay — the network <em>will</em> deliver your payment request twice, and only an
    idempotent handler turns that duplicate into a no-op; and <strong>distributed defence</strong>
    absorbs attacks that no single machine could survive. The 2016 Mirai botnet case still
    teaches the whole lesson: hundreds of thousands of weak-password IoT devices (cameras,
    routers) flooded DNS provider Dyn with junk traffic and took Twitter, Netflix and Reddit off
    the air for hours for much of the US East Coast. The vulnerability was thousands of independent
    nodes; the blast radius was a distributed system nobody had treated as one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the padlock icon means this site is safe." TLS proves the
    connection to <em>example-pharmacy.ru</em> is encrypted and that the server controls a
    certificate for that name — it says nothing about whether the operator is a pharmacy or a
    thief. Encryption protects the pipe; the request handling, business logic and data storage
    behind it are separate disciplines, which is why fully-encrypted sites get breached weekly.
  </p>

  <h2>Consensus: agreeing despite failures</h2>
  <p>
    Underneath replication sits the crown jewel: <strong>consensus</strong> — getting several
    machines to agree on one value despite crashes and lost messages, without ever splitting into
    two halves that each believe different things. Paxos (1998) solved it and became famous for
    being nearly incomprehensible; Raft (2014) re-solved it to be teachable: elect one leader,
    let it append log entries, require a majority (2 of 3, 3 of 5) to confirm each entry, and
    guarantee that any leader must possess every committed entry. The arithmetic is the safety
    argument: two majorities always overlap in at least one live node, so two conflicting
    decisions cannot both carry a majority. Databases you have heard of — etcd (which stores
    Kubernetes' entire cluster state), CockroachDB, Spanner — are Raft or Paxos with an engine
    attached. The cost of that safety is latency: every write must reach a majority before
    acknowledging, which is exactly why geo-distributed systems that cannot afford inter-continental
    round-trips on every write choose weaker consistency instead.
  </p>

  <p>
    The delivery-guarantee vocabulary repays memorising, because every message queue
    advertises it: <em>at-most-once</em> (send and forget — fast, lossy), <em>at-least-once</em>
    (retry until acknowledged — safe, but duplicates appear), and <em>exactly-once</em>
    (the marketing term for at-least-once delivery plus deduplication or transactional
    commit on the consumer side). The engineering consequence is universal: make your
    handlers idempotent — attach a request ID and ignore repeats — and you stop caring
    which guarantee the broker actually gives you.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Time is the deepest problem.</strong> Lamport's "Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of
    Events" (1978) showed that without shared time there is no global "happened before", and every
    consistency model is an answer to that hole. Spanner's approach is the industrial extreme:
    atomic clocks and GPS receivers in every datacentre give each event a confidence interval
    (TrueTime), so transactions can wait out the uncertainty and commit in a globally consistent
    order — physical time bought with hardware. The alternatives (logical clocks, hybrid
    timestamps, CRDTs that merge without any ordering) trade correctness guarantees for
    availability in different amounts, and choosing among them is the modern architect's main
    distributed decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tails eat the average.</strong> A service that calls 10 others, each 99.9%
    reliable, succeeds fully only about 99% of the time — 1% of a million requests per day is
    10,000 failures. Distributed engineering is therefore tail engineering: hedged requests sent
    to a second replica when the first is slow, backpressure instead of unbounded queues, timeouts
    tuned to measured distributions rather than wishes (Dean and Barroso's "The Tail at Scale" is
    the canonical treatment). Retry storms deserve special respect: a struggling service that
    receives three retries per failure is under 3× the load it was already failing under —
    which is why real systems add exponential backoff, jitter and circuit breakers that fail
    fast rather than multiplying the disaster.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open frontiers.</strong> Serverless platforms push the consistency question to its
    limit (stateless functions force you to externalise every bit of state, re-creating the
    problem per request); geo-replicated databases with tunable per-transaction consistency
    (CockroachDB, YugabyteDB) make the CAP choice a parameter rather than an architecture; and
    formal verification of consensus implementations is finally practical — TLA+ specifications
    caught real bugs in production systems at Amazon and MongoDB, because the failure modes of
    these algorithms are exactly the ones human intuition is worst at: interleavings, partial
    failure, and time behaving badly.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brewer CAP theorem (2000, proved 2012); Ongaro & Ousterhout, Raft (2014); Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications.</p>
</TopicPage>
